Figure 1: Dump of binary output file (block 0020).\page\fs20\par
Figure 1 shows the contents of block 0020 just after the assembly of the example file.  A description of each word is as follows:\line\line 00) 5340 - This is the first control word in the block which indicates the\par
    disposition of the next six data words.\par
\par
01) 0200 - All binary files automatically generate an opening *200 origin\par
    setting.  This is universally the case with all PDP-8 assemblers.\par
\par
02) 0200 - P?S/8 PAL outputs the prevailing current origin at the start of\par
    each binary output file.  In this example, this is redundant to the\par
    previous word only because this is the first (and only) output file.  The\par
    prevailing field setting is contained within the slurp loader; it is\par
    initialized to load into field zero by default.\par
\par
Note: Future releases of P?S/8 PAL may also include the prevailing field setting after the prevailing origin setting.  Future releases of P?S/8 BIN will include corresponding changes when converting binary paper-tapes to P/S/8 slurp format binary files.\par
\par
03) 6271 - The source program starts with a FIELD 7 directive; as such, a\par
    corresponding CDF 70 (6271) instruction is placed in the output file. \par
\par
Note: Most PDP-8 assemblers can place an automatic *200 after FIELD directives; this is generally not necessary as good programming practice demands the use of a new origin setting.  OS/8 PAL8 and P?S/8 PAL include option switches to enable this feature; by default this does not happen to minimize the binary file size.  Files intended to be punched to binary paper-tapes and ultimately loaded by the DEC "self-starting" binary loader must enable this feature to avoid a fatal loading bug which was never fixed.\par
\par
04) 1000 - The source program includes *1000 at this point.\par
\par
05) 7300 - The source program includes a CLA CLL instruction.\par
\par
06) 6203 - The source program includes a CIF CDF 00 instruction.\par
\par
07) 0125 - This is the second (and final) control word in the block (and\par
    entire program) which indicates the disposition of the next six data\par
    words.  (Actually, this is partially moot as the end-of-file is indicated\par
    within the control word.)\par
\par
10) 5603 - The program includes a JMP I 0203 instruction.\line\line 11) 7600 - The program includes the address value of 7600 used to exit the\par
    program.\par
\par
12) xxxx - The end-of-file point has been reached.  Words 012-175 are ignored.\par
    Additionally, words 176-177 are ignored in all slurp binary file blocks.\par
